part soc_5
  tally 2
  law
  note "SOC-5 · The old-age pension: retirement age 60 for women, 65 for men (.1); 19.50 % of each year's earnings credited (.2); the capital in year R sums each year's contribution times the VAL-IDX of every year from the next to R − 1, products exact, capital rounded (.3); the pension is the capital over the LE-<age> in force on the start day, age in whole years, rounded (.4); paid from the month after the claim, at the earliest the month after the age (.5); topped up to MIN-PENSION with 20 (women) or 25 (men) insured years (.6)."
  note "Gazette figures as rows (GEN-6): VAL-IDX-<yyyy> governing the year it is for, the LE table governing its in-force span, MIN-PENSION its span. Only the rows household D reads are written: VAL-IDX 1982–2018, LE-60 of the 2019 table, MIN-PENSION 2018–2026."

source soc_5 "Social Insurance Act, SOC-5 The old-age pension (tmp/cases/law/acts.md)"

index le_age = int 50..70

index soc5_year = year 1980..2030

input le_months[a in le_age]: num question "What is the life expectancy in months at this age in the table in force (LE-<age>, SOC-5 .4)?"

input min_pension: money DUK question "What is the minimum pension in force (MIN-PENSION, SOC-5 .6)?"

input soc5_age_men: int question "What is the retirement age for men (SOC-5 .1)?"

input soc5_age_women: int question "What is the retirement age for women (SOC-5 .1)?"

input soc5_contrib: num question "What share of a year's earnings is credited as pension contribution (SOC-5 .2)?"

input val_idx[y in soc5_year]: num question "What is the valorisation index for this year (VAL-IDX, SOC-5 .3)?"

